---

<DocPage
  title="API"
  eyebrow="Reference"
  path="api"
  lede="Everything 0.1.0 exports, with every option, every return value and the reasoning behind each default. Nothing on this page is planned: if it is here, it ships."
  description="Complete API reference for unruffled 0.1.0: useAction, useFailure, Announcer, useAnnounce, UnruffledProvider, usePrefersReducedMotion, the core machine runtime, the eight ports, the nine diagnostics and the testing harness."
  meta={[
    { label: "Version", value: "0.1.0" },
    { label: "React + core", value: "7.08 kB brotli" },
    { label: "Tests", value: "50" },
  ]}
>
  <Section id="surface" title="The whole surface">
    <p>
      Three published packages. No third-party runtime dependency in any of them:
      <code>@unruffled/react</code> depends on <code>@unruffled/core</code> and on nothing else, and React is a
      peer dependency at <code>^18</code> or <code>^19</code>. Node 20.19 or newer, ESM only.
    </p>

    <PropsTable rows={reactExports} nameHeading="@unruffled/react" />

    <p>
      The last five are marked advanced below. They exist so you can build your own primitive on the same
      machine runtime, not because a normal call site needs them.
    </p>

    <Callout tone="warning" title="If a hook is not in that table, it does not exist">
      <p>
        <code>useConfirm</code>, <code>useUndoable</code>, <code>useDataState</code>,
        <code>useSelection</code>, <code>useBulkAction</code>, <code>useRowState</code>,
        <code>usePartial</code>, <code>useServerErrors</code>, <code>useDirtyGuard</code> and
        <code>useGate</code> are named in the diagnostics and on the
        <a href={href("roadmap")}>roadmap</a>. None of them has shipped. Five of the nine rules name a fix you
        cannot apply yet, and only <code>UX1001</code> to <code>UX1004</code> are reported at all today, because
        the action machine is the only machine shipped. That is a known and deliberate state of this release.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </Section>

  <Section id="use-action" title="useAction">
    <p>
      <code>useAction&lt;T, A = void&gt;(run, options?)</code>. <code>run</code> receives the trigger arguments
      and an <code>AbortSignal</code>, and is replaced on every host render, so reading fresh state inside it
      is safe. The long-form page is
      <a href={href("components/use-action")}>here</a>; this is the table.
    </p>

    <h3>Options</h3>
    <PropsTable rows={actionOptions} />

    <h3>Result</h3>
    <PropsTable rows={actionResult} nameHeading="Value" />

    <h3>triggerProps</h3>
    <p>
      <code>getTriggerProps</code> accepts <code>args</code>, <code>onClick</code>,
      <code>onKeyDown</code> and <code>ref</code>. It accepts <code>disabled</code> only as
      <code>never</code>, which is a type error at the call site rather than a runtime surprise.
    </p>
    <PropsTable rows={triggerProps} nameHeading="Prop" />
  </Section>

  <Section id="use-failure" title="useFailure">
    <p>
      <code>useFailure(error: unknown, options?)</code> returns <code>FailureApi | null</code>. Pass
      <code>null</code> when there is no failure; the hook returns <code>null</code> and holds no subscriptions.
      The long-form page is
      <a href={href("components/use-failure")}>here</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>Options</h3>
    <PropsTable rows={failureOptions} />

    <h3>Result</h3>
    <PropsTable rows={failureResult} nameHeading="Value" />

    <h3>The ten kinds</h3>
    <p>
      Ordered from “not really a failure” to “nothing the user can do”. The default column is <code
        >canRetry</code
      >.
    </p>
    <PropsTable rows={failureKinds} nameHeading="Kind" />

    <p>
      Field errors are extracted from five conventions and normalised onto a dotted path: RFC 9457
      <code>errors</code> as a map or a list, JSON:API <code>source.pointer</code> with its RFC 6901 escapes, the
      <code>field_errors</code> and <code>fieldErrors</code> maps, a flat list of
      <code>path</code>, <code>field</code> or <code>name</code> with
      <code>message</code>, <code>detail</code> or <code>title</code>, and
      <code>violations</code>. A body is looked for at <code>body</code>, <code>data</code>,
      <code>response.data</code>, <code>response.body</code>, and finally on the error itself.
    </p>

    <Snippet code={defaultMessagesCode} lang="ts" name="default copy" />

    <Callout tone="note" title="Everything here is plain text">
      <p>
        No field ever contains markup, and no consumer should pass these strings to an HTML sink. Server <code
          >detail</code
        > text is carried through verbatim, which is exactly why it is kept separate from the user-facing <code
          >message</code
        >.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </Section>

  <Section id="announcer" title="Announcer and useAnnounce">
    <p>
      <code>Announcer</code> takes one optional prop, <code>id</code>, which prefixes the two rendered regions
      as <code>id-polite</code> and <code>id-assertive</code>. It defaults to no id, because nothing in the
      library looks these up and an unprefixed id risks colliding with the host application. The long-form
      page is <a href={href("components/announcer")}>here</a>.
    </p>

    <Snippet code={announcerCode} name="App.tsx" />
    <Snippet code={announcerMarkupCode} lang="html" name="rendered" />

    <p>
      Two regions rather than one, because switching a region's politeness at runtime is unreliable across
      screen readers. The message is routed instead: confirmations go to the polite region, failures to the
      assertive one. Both are <code>aria-atomic</code>, so a replaced message is read in full rather than
      diffed word by word. The registration happens in a layout effect, so an action that settles in the same
      commit as the mount does not announce into a void and trip UX1003 on first load.
    </p>

    <h3>useAnnounce</h3>
    <p>
      Returns <code>announce(message, politeness?)</code> and <code>live</code>. Politeness defaults to <code
        >"polite"</code
      >. The queue behind it enforces a 150 ms minimum gap between writes, drops a repeat of the same string
      within 500 ms, caps itself at 20 entries so a runaway loop cannot grow it without limit, and lets an
      assertive message jump ahead of anything waiting. Most code does not need this: action outcomes are
      announced by the machine. Reach for it when the application itself has something to say, such as
      “filters cleared, 240 results”.
    </p>
    <Snippet code={announceCode} lang="ts" name="useAnnounce" />
  </Section>

  <Section id="provider" title="UnruffledProvider">
    <p>
      Optional. Every hook works without it, because a library that only behaves correctly once a provider is
      wired has made its first-run experience the thing most likely to be got wrong.
    </p>

    <PropsTable rows={providerProps} nameHeading="Prop" />

    <h3>UnruffledDefaults</h3>
    <p>
      Deliberately behavioural only. There is nothing here about colour, radius, spacing or typography,
      because the library emits no visual and so has no opinion to configure. Note what is absent: <code
        >guard</code
      >, <code>focus</code> and <code>resetAfterMs</code> are per-call-site decisions and cannot be set globally.
    </p>
    <PropsTable rows={providerDefaults} />

    <p>
      <code>useUnruffledDefaults()</code> reads this back, returning an empty object when there is no provider.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="reduced-motion" title="usePrefersReducedMotion">
    <p>
      <code>usePrefersReducedMotion(): boolean</code>, and
      <code>prefersReducedMotion(): boolean</code> for code outside a component. The hook subscribes to the media
      query through <code>useSyncExternalStore</code> rather than reading
      <code>matchMedia</code> once into state at mount, so a user who turns the preference on mid-session, or moves
      a window to a display with different settings, gets the new answer without a remount.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is nothing to enable. The library treats <code>true</code> as “reduce” everywhere without being asked,
      and reduction means amplitude, not a kill switch: a countdown keeps counting and stays accurate, it simply
      stops animating. On the server and in a runtime without <code>matchMedia</code> the answer is <code
        >false</code
      >, which keeps the server output stable and corrects itself on hydration. See <a
        href={href("animation")}>Animation</a
      >.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="advanced" title="Advanced: building your own primitive">
    <p>
      These five are exported so that a primitive the library has not shipped yet can be built on the same
      runtime, with the same ports and the same testability. They are not needed for ordinary use and their
      shapes may move before 1.0.
    </p>
    <PropsTable rows={advanced} nameHeading="Export" />
  </Section>

  <Section id="core" title="@unruffled/core">
    <p>
      Nothing in core imports a framework, touches a global or emits a pixel. A framework adapter drives <code
        >createService</code
      > and renders whatever the matching
      <code>connect</code> function returns.
    </p>

    <h3>The machine runtime</h3>
    <PropsTable rows={coreMachineExports} nameHeading="Export" />

    <p>
      Types exported alongside them: <code>ActionFn</code>, <code>AnyEvent</code>,
      <code>EffectApi</code>, <code>EffectFn</code>, <code>GuardFn</code>,
      <code>InternalEvent</code>, <code>MachineApi</code>, <code>MachineDefinition</code>,
      <code>Service</code>, <code>ServiceOptions</code>, <code>Snapshot</code>,
      <code>StateNode</code>, <code>Transition</code>, <code>TransitionConfig</code> and
      <code>TransitionRecord</code>, plus the action machine's own
      <code>ActionApi</code>, <code>ActionAttrs</code>, <code>ActionStatus</code>,
      <code>ActionAnnouncements</code>, <code>ActionContext</code>, <code>ActionEvent</code>,
      <code>ActionProps</code>, <code>ActionStateValue</code>, <code>FocusPolicy</code>,
      <code>GuardMode</code>, <code>PendingPolicy</code>, <code>RetryPolicy</code> and
      <code>StashedOutcome</code>.
    </p>

    <h3>The eight ports</h3>
    <p>
      Ports are the only way a machine reaches the outside world. Every impure capability is declared as a
      narrow interface and injected, which is what makes timing deterministic in tests, multi-window and
      shadow-DOM correctness structural rather than remembered, and a Vue or Svelte adapter a mechanical job.
      No port is mandatory.
    </p>
    <PropsTable rows={corePortExports} nameHeading="Port" />

    <h3>Everything else</h3>
    <PropsTable rows={coreOtherExports} nameHeading="Export" />
  </Section>

  <Section id="diagnostics" title="@unruffled/core/diagnostics">
    <p>
      A separate subpath, not part of the barrel. Everything behind it is wrapped in
      <code>__DEV__</code>, which the default build replaces with <code>false</code>, so the rule table, the
      formatter and every call site collapse to nothing and a production bundle carries no diagnostics bytes
      at all. The <code>development</code> export condition resolves to the build where <code>__DEV__</code> is
      <code>true</code>.
    </p>
    <PropsTable rows={diagnosticsExports} nameHeading="Export" />
    <p>
      Reports are de-duplicated one per code per call site, so a rule inside a list row fires once rather than
      once per row, and history is capped at 200 events. The nine rules, with what each one costs a user, are
      on the <a href={href("diagnostics")}>diagnostics</a> pages.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="testing" title="@unruffled/testing">
    <p>
      A library whose whole value is correct timing cannot be allowed to fail at timing, and a suite that
      sleeps will not catch it. Everything here exists so that “the spinner does not appear before 150 ms”,
      “the second click was swallowed” and “exactly one announcement reached the live region” are ordinary
      assertions. Install it as a devDependency.
    </p>
    <PropsTable rows={testingExports} nameHeading="Export" />
    <Snippet code={testCode} lang="ts" name="action.test.ts" />
  </Section>

  <Section id="subpaths" title="Subpath exports and what each costs">
    <p>
      Brotli, measured by <code>size-limit</code> against the built output. The React layer figures exclude React
      itself; the barrel figure is everything retained, which is the number to judge the library by rather than
      a best case with most of it shaken out.
    </p>
    <PropsTable rows={subpaths} nameHeading="Subpath" />
    <p>
      Both packages set <code>sideEffects: false</code> and ship ESM only. The per-primitive subpaths are a guarantee
      rather than a workaround: the barrel tree-shakes, and the subpaths exist so you can prove it in your own bundle
      report.
    </p>
  </Section>
</DocPage>
